# Cursor Rules for Playwright E2E Test Generation

## Architecture Overview

This project uses a **fixture-based auto-login pattern** combined with **data-driven testing** for Playwright e2e tests. All tests automatically authenticate before execution, and test cases are defined as data structures rather than individual test functions.

## Core Principles

### 1. Fixture-Based Auto-Login
- **ALWAYS** import `test` and `expect` from `./fixtures`, NOT from `@playwright/test`
- The custom fixture automatically handles authentication before each test
- Tests receive an authenticated `page` automatically - never call `login()` manually in tests
- Exception: Only use base `test` from `@playwright/test` when testing the login flow itself

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';

test('my test', async ({ page }) => {
  // page is already authenticated
});

// ❌ WRONG
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
await login(page); // Don't do this - fixture handles it
```

### 2. Data-Driven Testing Pattern
- **ALWAYS** use data-driven approach when multiple test cases follow the same pattern
- Define test cases as an array of objects with test data
- Use a loop to generate tests dynamically
- Separate test data from test logic

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Data-driven
const testCases = [
  { project: 'Web Application', taskName: 'Task 1', column: 'To Do', tags: ['Feature'] },
  { project: 'Web Application', taskName: 'Task 2', column: 'In Progress', tags: ['Bug'] },
];

for (const testCase of testCases) {
  test(`Verify ${testCase.taskName}...`, async ({ page }) => {
    // Test logic using testCase data
  });
}

// ❌ WRONG - Repetitive individual tests
test('test 1', async ({ page }) => { /* ... */ });
test('test 2', async ({ page }) => { /* ... */ });
```

### 3. Helper Functions
- **ALWAYS** extract reusable operations into helper functions
- Place helper functions at the top of the test file (before test data)
- Use semantic selectors in helpers (getByRole, getByLabel, getByText)
- Keep helpers pure and focused on single responsibilities

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
async function navigateToProject(page: Page, projectName: string) {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: new RegExp(`^${projectName}`) }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('banner').getByRole('heading', { name: projectName })).toBeVisible();
}

// Use in tests
await navigateToProject(page, testCase.project);
```

### 4. Semantic Selectors
- **ALWAYS** use semantic selectors: `getByRole`, `getByLabel`, `getByText`, `getByPlaceholder`
- **NEVER** use CSS selectors like `.class-name` or `#id` unless absolutely necessary
- Prefer `getByRole` for interactive elements
- Use `getByLabel` for form fields
- Use `getByText` for text content

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
await page.getByLabel('Username').fill('admin');
await expect(page.getByText('Success', { exact: true })).toBeVisible();

// ❌ WRONG
await page.locator('.btn-primary').click();
await page.locator('#username').fill('admin');
```

### 5. Test Isolation
- Each test is completely isolated - no shared state between tests
- Each test gets a fresh authenticated page from the fixture
- Tests can run in parallel safely
- Use `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'parallel' })` if needed for parallel execution within a file

### 6. Type Safety
- **ALWAYS** use TypeScript types for test data
- Use `as const` for test data arrays to ensure type safety
- Import types from `@playwright/test`: `Page`, `Locator`, `Expect`

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
import type { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';

const testCases = [
  { project: 'Web Application', taskName: 'Task', column: 'To Do', tags: ['Feature'] },
] as const;

async function helper(page: Page, name: string): Promise<Locator> {
  // ...
}
```

## File Structure

```
tests/
├── fixtures.ts          # Custom fixtures with auto-login - DO NOT MODIFY
├── utils/
│   └── auth.ts          # Login helper - DO NOT MODIFY
└── *.spec.ts            # Test files - ADD NEW TESTS HERE
```

## Creating New Test Files

### Step 1: Import from fixtures
```typescript
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';
import type { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';
```

### Step 2: Define helper functions (if needed)
```typescript
async function myHelper(page: Page, param: string) {
  // Implementation using semantic selectors
}
```

### Step 3: Define test data (if data-driven)
```typescript
const testCases = [
  { field1: 'value1', field2: 'value2' },
  { field1: 'value3', field2: 'value4' },
] as const;
```

### Step 4: Write tests
```typescript
// For data-driven tests
for (const testCase of testCases) {
  test(`Test: ${testCase.field1}`, async ({ page }) => {
    // Test logic
  });
}

// For one-off tests
test('unique test scenario', async ({ page }) => {
  // Test logic
});
```

## Test Data Structure Guidelines

When creating test data arrays:
- Use descriptive field names that match what they represent
- Keep data flat and simple - avoid nested objects unless necessary
- Use arrays for multiple values (e.g., tags: ['Feature', 'High Priority'])
- Add `as const` for type safety
- Group related test cases together

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD structure
const testCases = [
  {
    project: 'Web Application',
    taskName: 'Implement feature',
    column: 'To Do',
    tags: ['Feature', 'High Priority'],
  },
] as const;
```

## Helper Function Guidelines

When creating helper functions:
- Name functions descriptively: `navigateToProject`, `verifyTaskExists`, `getColumnSection`
- Use async/await for all Playwright operations
- Return Locators when appropriate for chaining
- Keep functions focused - one responsibility per function
- Use semantic selectors inside helpers
- Add type annotations for parameters and return types

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD helper
async function navigateToProject(page: Page, projectName: string): Promise<void> {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: new RegExp(`^${projectName}`) }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('banner').getByRole('heading', { name: projectName })).toBeVisible();
}

// ✅ GOOD helper returning Locator
function getColumnSection(page: Page, columnName: string): Locator {
  return page.getByRole('heading', { name: new RegExp(`^${columnName}`) }).locator('..');
}
```

## Assertions

- **ALWAYS** use `expect` from fixtures (not base expect)
- Use appropriate matchers: `toBeVisible()`, `toHaveText()`, `toContainText()`, etc.
- Use `exact: true` when matching text exactly
- Use soft assertions (`expect.soft()`) when you want to check multiple things without stopping on first failure

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Projects' })).toBeVisible();
await expect(element.getByText('Feature', { exact: true })).toBeVisible();

// Soft assertions for multiple checks
await expect.soft(page.getByTestId('status')).toHaveText('Success');
await expect.soft(page.getByTestId('count')).toHaveText('5');
```

## Test Organization

- Group related tests in the same file
- Use descriptive test names that explain what is being tested
- Include context in test names: project, feature, expected behavior
- Keep tests focused - one scenario per test

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD test names
test('Verify task exists in Web Application project', async ({ page }) => { });
test('Verify task tags are displayed correctly', async ({ page }) => { });

// ❌ BAD test names
test('test 1', async ({ page }) => { });
test('check stuff', async ({ page }) => { });
```

## Avoiding Common Mistakes

### ❌ DON'T:
- Import `test` from `@playwright/test` (use `./fixtures`)
- Call `login()` manually in tests
- Use CSS selectors (`.class`, `#id`)
- Create repetitive individual test functions when data-driven would work
- Share state between tests
- Use `test.step()` in fixtures (not supported)
- Hardcode credentials in tests (they're in `utils/auth.ts`)

### ✅ DO:
- Import `test` from `./fixtures`
- Let fixtures handle authentication
- Use semantic selectors
- Use data-driven approach for similar test cases
- Keep tests isolated
- Extract reusable logic to helpers
- Use TypeScript types

## When to Use Data-Driven vs Individual Tests

**Use data-driven when:**
- Multiple tests follow the same pattern
- Only test data changes between tests
- Testing same functionality with different inputs
- You have 3+ similar test cases

**Use individual tests when:**
- Test has completely different flow
- One-off test scenario
- Test requires custom setup/teardown
- Test logic varies significantly

## Example: Complete Test File Template

```typescript
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';
import type { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';

// Helper functions
async function navigateToProject(page: Page, projectName: string) {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: new RegExp(`^${projectName}`) }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('banner').getByRole('heading', { name: projectName })).toBeVisible();
}

function getColumnSection(page: Page, columnName: string): Locator {
  return page.getByRole('heading', { name: new RegExp(`^${columnName}`) }).locator('..');
}

// Test data
const testCases = [
  {
    project: 'Web Application',
    taskName: 'Task Name',
    column: 'To Do',
    tags: ['Feature'],
  },
] as const;

// Tests
for (const testCase of testCases) {
  test(`Verify ${testCase.taskName} in ${testCase.project}`, async ({ page }) => {
    await navigateToProject(page, testCase.project);
    const columnSection = getColumnSection(page, testCase.column);
    await expect(columnSection.getByRole('heading', { name: testCase.taskName })).toBeVisible();
  });
}
```

## CI/CD Considerations

- Tests run with 2 shards per browser (configured in `.github/workflows/playwright.yml`)
- Blob reports are automatically merged
- Traces are included in blob reports (no separate upload needed)
- Tests run in parallel by default
- Each test is isolated and can run independently

## Debugging

- Use `trace: 'retain-on-failure'` (already configured)
- View traces: `npx playwright show-trace <trace-file>`
- View HTML report: `npx playwright show-report`
- All traces are included in merged blob reports

